+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. Two edits that started from the same state of a visit therefore reach the record store at different times, and the store has to say what the visit's state is afterwards. Arrival order says nothing about which worker is right.
+
+Every option keeps both original submissions; that is a project tenet and does not distinguish them. What differs is what the server does with the contents of the two edits, and that choice sets the shape of three things outside this node: what a tablet must remember and send, what coordinators review, and which visits may enter the Friday CSV for the science partner. A wrong choice shows up either as a review queue two coordinators cannot clear, or as exported records that no worker actually observed.
+
+A visit's fields are not independent. A species identification and its cover estimate are one observation spread over two values; an observer correction and a newly added species are unrelated. The record format does not mark which is which.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Undetected combination of related edits (major).** Whether the store can produce a visit that mixes two workers' scientifically related values without a person having looked. Derives from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and from the non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*. An error of this kind reaches the science partner looking like a normal record.
+- **Review workload (major).** How many visits wait for one of two coordinators, and so how many are held out of a Friday export. Derives from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate* and the goal *make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*.
+- **Maintained rules (moderate).** How much behavior has to be kept correct as the visit format changes, by a team with no dedicated systems administrator. Derives from the same coordinator tenet.
+- **Explaining an exported value (moderate).** How much a coordinator has to read to answer "where did this value come from?". Derives from the inspectability tenet.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size (minor).** At about 6,000 visits a season on 12 tablets, neither option strains a tablet; this matters only as a tie-breaker.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every submission is a complete visit and names the revision the worker's copy started from. If that revision is still the visit's current one, the submission becomes current. If it is not, the store does not look inside either visit: the two stand as competing revisions and the visit waits for a coordinator, who compares them as whole visits and decides. Two workers who touched entirely different fields still produce review work.
+
+- Undetected combination of related edits: **strong**. The store never builds a visit out of parts of two submissions. Any combined visit is written by a named coordinator.
+- Review workload: **weak**. Every pair of concurrent edits to one visit needs a coordinator, including pairs that changed unrelated fields, and the visit is out of the export until someone acts.
+- Maintained rules: **strong**. The outcome depends only on revision IDs. Adding or reshaping a visit field changes nothing in the concurrency behavior.
+- Explaining an exported value: **strong**. An exported visit is exactly one worker's submission or one coordinator's resolution; the history is a chain of whole revisions.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. A tablet keeps one revision ID per local visit, but sends the complete visit on every edit.
+
+Summary: no merge logic and no machine-made records, paid for in coordinator time.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every submission supplies the state it started from and the changes made to it. The server compares both edits against their common starting state. Where they changed different fields, it combines them into a new current visit without review; where they changed the same field, the overlap goes to a coordinator. Rules define what "the same field" means for scalars, for the observer list, and for species entries, and can be written to treat a species entry (identification and cover together) as one unit.
+
+- Undetected combination of related edits: **weak**. Relationships the rules encode are respected; relationships they do not encode are combined silently, and the result is a visit no worker submitted. The format cannot enumerate every relationship, for example between a species change and a note that refers to it.
+- Review workload: **strong**. Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator. More visits are export-ready on Friday without anyone acting.
+- Maintained rules: **weak**. Each field type needs a merge rule, list fields need identity rules for their entries, and every change to the visit format reopens them.
+- Explaining an exported value: **partial**. The value is traceable, but only through field-level provenance: which submission each field of a combined visit came from.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. Submissions carry only changes, but a tablet keeps a full starting snapshot of every visit it holds.
+
+Summary: a smaller queue and fuller exports, paid for in rules that must stay right and in records combined without a reader.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+The two major criteria point in opposite directions, so the selection is a choice between them. A long review queue is visible: coordinators see it, the held-out visits can be counted, and the remedy is their time. A visit wrongly combined by a rule is not visible to anyone until the science partner or a later survey contradicts it. The project prefers the failure it can see. That is the inspectability tenet taking precedence over the convenience half of the two-coordinator tenet, and the moderate criteria both fall on the same side.
+
+### What this commits the other nodes to
+
+Tablet-sync:
+
+- Keep, for every visit held locally, the ID of the revision the local copy started from, and send it with each edit.
+- Send complete visits. No starting snapshots and no change lists are needed.
+- Tell a worker when their accepted edit is waiting for a coordinator rather than standing as the visit's state, and show the revisions it is competing with. An edit in that position is saved, not lost and not rejected, and the interface has to make that distinction.
+- Let work continue on a visit that is waiting for review.
+
+Review-export:
+
+- Provide a screen that compares competing revisions of one visit as whole visits. Showing which fields differ is a display aid; the coordinator rules on the visit.
+- Treat the set of visits with competing revisions as the coordinators' queue, and expect it to include pairs that differ only in unrelated fields.
+- Hold every visit that is waiting for review out of the CSV, and make the held-out visits visible before Friday so that an incomplete export is a known quantity.
+- Handle a visit that was exported in an earlier week and has since acquired a competing revision. A late tablet can put any visit back into review.
+- Field-level provenance is not needed: an exported visit has one source.
+
+### When to reconsider
+
+The merge option was not eliminated; it lost on a weighing that depends on facts a season of use will supply. Reopen the choice if:
+
+- Visits waiting for review regularly outnumber what two coordinators clear before Friday, so that exports go out materially incomplete.
+- Coordinators find that most competing revisions differ in unrelated fields and resolve them the same mechanical way each time. That pattern is evidence a rule could do the work, and shows which rule.
+- The visit format gains an explicit way to mark values that belong together, which removes the main weakness of merging.
+
+Reconsidering does not require discarding anything collected under this decision. The store holds every revision complete, with the ID of the revision it started from, so the changes each worker made and their common starting state can be derived from stored history. A merge introduced later could run over the same records, and tablets would not have to change what they send.
